        0.10.1\\nscreen-resolution-extra  0.0.0\\nSecretStorage            3.3.3\\nsetuptools               68.1.2\\nsix                      1.16.0\\nSQLAlchemy               1.4.50\\nssh-import-id            5.11\\nsystemd-python           235\\ntestresources            2.0.1\\nubuntu-drivers-common    0.0.0\\nubuntu-pro-client        8001\\nufw                      0.36.2\\nunattended-upgrades      0.1\\nunittest2                1.1.0\\nuritemplate              4.1.1\\nurllib3                  2.0.7\\nusb-creator              0.3.16\\nwadllib                  1.3.6\\nwebencodings             0.5.1\\nwheel                    0.42.0\\nxdg                      5\\nxkit                     0.0.0\\nyubikey-manager          5.2.1\\nzipp                     1.0.0\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "14f9b1b1-1faf-4a8c-84b0-e0397e2003df", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 13}
{"id": "11a2dcad-8ee8-4397-8acc-df713b215c35", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T06:19:45.589594", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "bb44abfc-3ed5-478a-8586-cdcf5f4ef334", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "### Proposed Experiment Design Plan\n\n#### 1. Research Objective & Experimental Framework\nThe goal is to determine how varying the runtime clipping ratio affects the perplexity (PPL) of **LLAMA2-7B** under **symmetric input quantization** and **asymmetric KV cache quantization**, and to identify the optimal fixed clipping ratio $\\alpha^*$ that minimizes perplexity by balancing quantization rounding noise against saturation clipping error.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Experimental Variables & Quantization Formulation\n- **Independent Variable:**\n  - Clipping ratio $\\alpha$: A scaling factor applied to determine clipping thresholds for dynamic runtime quantization (e.g., $T_{\\text{clip}} = \\alpha \\cdot X_{\\max}$).\n  - Candidate Sweep Range & Granularity: $\\alpha \\in [0.70, 1.00]$ with fine-grained steps (e.g., step size $\\Delta \\alpha = 0.02$ or $0.05$, spanning $0.70, 0.75, 0.80, 0.85, 0.90, 0.92, 0.94, 0.96, 0.98, 1.00$).\n- **Quantization Pipeline & Settings:**\n  - **Inputs (Dynamic Activations):** Symmetric uniform quantization (e.g., INT4 or INT8), mapping $[-\\alpha \\cdot \\max(|X|), \\alpha \\cdot \\max(|X|)]$ to $[-2^{b-1}+1, 2^{b-1}-1]$ (or $[-2^{b-1}, 2^{b-1}-1]$) with zero-point $Z=0$.\n  - **KV Cache:** Asymmetric uniform quantization (e.g., INT4 or INT8 per-token / per-channel / per-head) mapping dynamic range $[X_{\\min}(\\alpha), X_{\\max}(\\alpha)]$ to $[0, 2^b-1]$ with scale $S$ and non-zero zero-point $Z$.\n  - **Weights:** Kept in original precision (FP16) or standard fixed INT4/INT8 to isolate activation/KV cache quantization effects.\n- **Dependent Variables:**\n  - Primary Metric: Perplexity (PPL) measured via sliding window / standard cross-entropy loss over evaluation text.\n  - Secondary Diagnostics: Layer-wise Quantization Mean Squared Error (MSE), signal-to-quantization-noise ratio (SQNR), and clipping saturation fraction (% clipped values).\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Datasets & Evaluation Protocols\n- **Primary Language Modeling Benchmarks:**\n  - **WikiText-2** (test split, standard 2048 sequence length).\n  - **C4 (Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus)** (validation split, 2048 sequence length) to assess generalizability across domains.\n- **Evaluation Protocol:**\n  - Full-sequence autoregressive causal language modeling without training or fine-tuning (post-training runtime inference).\n  - Compute average negative log-likelihood per token exponentiated to yield PPL.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Baseline Controls & Comparative Conditions\n1. **Full-Precision (FP16) Baseline:** Establishes the unquantized theoretical lower bound for PPL on LLAMA2-7B.\n2. **Unclipped Quantized Baseline ($\\alpha = 1.00$):** Standard dynamic min-max quantization without clipping to measure the exact gain obtained by introducing clipping.\n3. **Severe Clipping Control ($\\alpha \\le 0.70$):** Demonstrates the failure mode where clipping saturation error dominates quantization rounding benefits.\n4. **Isolated Modality Controls:** Testing clipping on inputs alone vs. KV cache alone vs. joint clipping to disentangle sensitivity.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. Analysis & Optimization Plan\n- **U-Curve Characterization:** Plot PPL as a function of $\\alpha$. Identify the convex minimum corresponding to $\\alpha^*$.\n- **Error Decomposition Analysis:** Analyze the trade-off curve between:\n  - *Quantization step size / rounding error:* Decreases monotonically as $\\alpha \\to 0$ (smaller range $\\Rightarrow$ finer grid resolution $\\Delta$).\n  - *Clipping distortion / truncation error:* Increases monotonically as $\\alpha \\to 0$ (more outlier mass truncated to boundary values).\n- **Global vs. Layer-wise Stability:** Verify whether a single global fixed $\\alpha^*$ maintains stability across all transformer layers or if sensitivity diverges across early vs. late attention layers.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. Inquiries on Source Setup Details\nTo align this experimental design with the exact source methodology (NeurIPS 2024 paper 94328), could you clarify the source-supported specifications for:\n- **`neurips2024/94328/2:setup_1`**: Exact bit-width configuration (e.g., W4A4KV4, W8A8KV8, or INT4 KV cache with INT8 inputs) and granularity (per-tensor, per-token, or per-channel/head)?\n- **`neurips2024/94328/2:setup_10`**: The precise clipping formulation (e.g., whether the fixed clipping ratio is shared across inputs and KV cache, or applied per-tensor / per-token) and the tested candidate range?\n- **`neurips2024/94328/2:setup_17`**: Evaluation benchmarks, token sequence lengths, and baseline reference values reported in the study?", "evidence": "Task constraints specify LLAMA2-7B as the base model, symmetric input quantization, asymmetric KV cache quantization, and perplexity (PPL) as the primary evaluation metric. Repository inspection confirmed that this is a design-only task with no compute or pre-existing code repository required. Topic identifiers (setup_1, setup_10, setup_17) are available for source-supported clarification.", "assumptions": ["Model architecture under investigation is fixed to LLAMA2-7B (meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf).", "Quantization is applied dynamically at runtime during inference without re-training or weight parameter updates (post-training quantization setting).", "Input activations use symmetric quantization, while KV cache representations use asymmetric quantization with scale and zero-point.", "The clipping ratio alpha scales the dynamic activation/KV cache range before computing scale and zero-point factors."], "uncertainties": ["Precise bit-width assignment across weights, inputs, and KV cache (e.g. 4-bit vs 8-bit) as defined in setup_1.", "Exact mathematical parameterization and search grid for the fixed clipping ratio as defined in setup_10.", "Specific evaluation corpus splits and context lengths d