{"id": "3c9022d1-348f-4369-b727-9cad7f6e84bd", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T06:19:55.552331", "source": "user", "parent_id": "873c4d5c-1b95-43d7-af12-2439da66ef89", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"The proposed plan is sound and approved. Regarding the setup queries:\\n- As confirmed by our fixed requirements, the study centers on **LLAMA2-7B** (`neurips2024/94328/2:setup_1`), evaluates using **Perplexity (PPL)** (`neurips2024/94328/2:setup_17`), and applies **asymmetric quantization** to cached key/value tensors (`neurips2024/94328/2:setup_10`) alongside symmetric input quantization.\\n- Specific bit-widths (e.g., INT4 or INT8 for KV cache and inputs), benchmark text corpora (such as WikiText-2 and C4), and the exact parameter grid for the clipping ratio are flexible experimental design choices for you to specify and justify, as empirical outcomes and unstated setup details are unknown.\\n\\nPlease proceed to provide the comprehensive final experiment design satisfying the deliverable contract:\\n1. Detailed experimental setup, mathematical formulation of clipping and quantization (scale, zero-point, symmetric vs. asymmetric).\\n2. Clipping ratio sweep design and justification.\\n3. Baselines, controls, and ablation configurations.\\n4. Evaluation protocol, benchmark datasets, and sequence parameters.\\n5. Analysis plan explaining how the trade-off between clipping distortion and rounding error determines the optimal fixed clipping ratio.\\n6. Feasibility considerations.\\n\\nRemember this is a design-only task; do not fabricate experimental results or claim execution.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
{"id": "99865a42-8c5f-4dd3-8769-42dea9e51930", "timestamp": "2026-10-18T06:20:42.759599", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "3c9022d1-348f-4369-b727-9cad7f6e84bd", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Comprehensive Experiment Design: Impact of Runtime Clipping Ratio on LLAMA2-7B Quantization Perplexity\n\n## 1. Executive Summary & Experimental Objectives\nThis experiment designs a rigorous, controlled protocol to evaluate how runtime clipping ratio ($\\alpha$) affects language modeling perplexity (PPL) on **LLAMA2-7B** under a hybrid quantization scheme: **symmetric dynamic input (activation) quantization** and **asymmetric KV cache quantization**.\n\nThe core objective is to map the trade-off curve between quantization rounding noise (which decreases as the representation range contracts) and clipping saturation distortion (which increases as extreme values are truncated), thereby determining the optimal fixed clipping ratio $\\alpha^*$ that minimizes perplexity during autoregressive inference.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mathematical Formulation of Quantization and Clipping\n\n### 2.1 Model Specifications\n- **Target Model:** LLAMA2-7B (`meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf`).\n  - Architecture: 32 Transformer layers, hidden dimension $d = 4096$, intermediate dimension $11008$, 32 attention heads ($d_h = 128$), RoPE positional embeddings, SwiGLU non-linearities, Grouped Query Attention (GQA) is standard MHA in 7B.\n  - Base Precision for Weights: Kept in original FP16 (or optionally fixed unclipped W8A8 baseline) to isolate the causal impact of activation and KV cache quantization.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Symmetric Input Quantization (Dynamic Activations)\nInput activations $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{B \\times L \\times d}$ entering linear projections (e.g., $W_q, W_k, W_v, W_o, W_{\\text{gate}}, W_{\\text{up}}, W_{\\text{down}}$) exhibit zero-centered distributions with sparse, large-magnitude outlier channels.\n\n1. **Unclipped Dynamic Range:**\n   $$X_{\\max} = \\max_{j} |X_j| \\quad (\\text{per-token or per-tensor})$$\n2. **Clipped Dynamic Threshold with Clipping Ratio $\\alpha_X \\in (0, 1]$:**\n   $$T_X(\\alpha_X) = \\alpha_X \\cdot X_{\\max}$$\n3. **Clamping Operation:**\n   $$\\bar{X} = \\text{clamp}(X, -T_X(\\alpha_X), T_X(\\alpha_X))$$\n4. **Scale Calculation (Symmetric, Zero-point $Z_X = 0$):**\n   For bit-width $b_{\\text{in}}$ (primary target $b_{\\text{in}} = 8$ or $4$, mapped to integer interval $[-2^{b_{\\text{in}}-1}, 2^{b_{\\text{in}}-1}-1]$ or $[-2^{b_{\\text{in}}-1}+1, 2^{b_{\\text{in}}-1}-1]$):\n   $$S_X = \\frac{T_X(\\alpha_X)}{2^{b_{\\text{in}}-1} - 1}$$\n5. **Quantization and Dequantization:**\n   $$Q_X(X) = \\text{round}\\left(\\frac{\\bar{X}}{S_X}\\right), \\quad \\hat{X} = Q_X(X) \\cdot S_X$$\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Asymmetric KV Cache Quantization\nKey ($K$) and Value ($V$) tensors stored in the KV cache across decoding steps exhibit non-zero-centered, skewed distributions (e.g., post-RoPE Key vectors and post-activation Value vectors often exhibit asymmetric positive/negative channel bounds). Asymmetric quantization maps the dynamic interval $[V_{\\min}, V_{\\max}]$ into unsigned integer interval $[0, 2^{b_{KV}} - 1]$.\n\n1. **Unclipped Bounds:**\n   For a given key/value vector (per-head per-token or per-channel vector $Y \\in \\{K, V\\}$):\n   $$Y_{\\min} = \\min(Y), \\quad Y_{\\max} = \\max(Y)$$\n   $$\\text{Range}(Y) = Y_{\\max} - Y_{\\min}, \\quad \\text{Midpoint}(Y) = \\frac{Y_{\\max} + Y_{\\min}}{2}$$\n\n2. **Clipped Dynamic Range with Clipping Ratio $\\alpha_{KV} \\in (0, 1]$:**\n   To contract the dynamic range symmetrically around the distribution center:\n   $$\\Delta_{\\text{half}} = \\alpha_{KV} \\cdot \\frac{\\text{Range}(Y)}{2}$$\n   $$T_{Y,\\min}(\\alpha_{KV}) = \\text{Midpoint}(Y) - \\Delta_{\\text{half}}$$\n   $$T_{Y,\\max}(\\alpha_{KV}) = \\text{Midpoint}(Y) + \\Delta_{\\text{half}}$$\n\n3. **Clamping Operation:**\n   $$\\bar{Y} = \\text{clamp}\\left(Y, T_{Y,\\min}(\\alpha_{KV}), T_{Y,\\max}(\\alpha_{KV})\\right)$$\n\n4. **Scale and Asymmetric Zero-Point ($Z_{KV}$):**\n   For bit-width $b_{KV}$ (primary target $b_{KV} = 4$ for high-throughput memory reduction, and $b_{KV} = 8$):\n   $$S_Y = \\frac{T_{Y,\\max}(\\alpha_{KV}) - T_{Y,\\min}(\\alpha_{KV})}{2^{b_{KV}} - 1} = \\frac{\\alpha_{KV} \\cdot (Y_{\\max} - Y_{\\min})}{2^{b_{KV}} - 1}$$\n   $$Z_Y = \\text{round}\\left( - \\frac{T_{Y,\\min}(\\alpha_{KV})}{S_Y} \\right) = \\text{round}\\left( \\frac{2^{b_{KV}} - 1}{2} - \\frac{\\text{Midpoint}(Y)}{S_Y} \\right)$$\n   $$Z_Y = \\text{clamp}\\left(Z_Y, 0, 2^{b_{KV}} - 1\\right)$$\n\n5. **Quantization and Dequantization:**\n   $$Q_Y(Y) = \\text{clamp}\\left(\\text{round}\\left(\\frac{\\bar{Y} - T_{Y,\\min}}{S_Y}\\right), 0, 2^{b_{KV}} - 1\\right)$$\n   $$\\hat{Y} = S_Y \\cdot (Q_Y(Y) - Z_Y)$$\n\n---\n\n## 3. Clipping Ratio Sweep Design & Parameter Justification\n\n### 3.1 Candidate Grid and Resolution\nTo isolate the convex minimum $\\alpha^*$ without overlooking boundary phenomena, we formulate a two-tier dense grid:\n\n| Regime | Ratio Values ($\\alpha$) | Step Size ($\\Delta \\alpha$) | Justification |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Coarse Lower Bound** | $0.70, 0.75, 0.80$ | $0.05$ | Tests catastrophic saturation threshold where clipping truncation dominates. |\n| **Fine Search Region** | $0.85, 0.88, 0.90, 0.92, 0.94, 0.95, 0.96, 0.98$ | $0.01 - 0.02$ | High-sensitivity region where optimal trade-off between outlier preservation and step-size reduction occurs. |\n| **Upper Bound (Control)** | $1.00$ | — | Standard unclipped min-max quantization baseline. |\n\n### 3.2 Coupling Strategy\n1. **Primary Protocol (Joint Fixed Ratio):**\n   Apply a uniform global clipping ratio $\\alpha = \\alpha_X = \\alpha_{KV}$ across both input activations and KV cache. This directly addresses the research question of identifying a single optimal fixed runtime parameter.\n2. **Factorial Secondary Protocol (Decoupled Grid):**\n   A $4 \\times 4$ factorial sweep testing $\\alpha_X \\in \\{0.85, 0.90, 0.95, 1.00\\}$ against $\\alpha_{KV} \\in \\{0.85, 0.90, 0.95, 1.00\\}$ to determine whether activation inputs or KV cache representations are more sensitive to saturation vs rounding noise.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Baselines, Controls, and Ablation Configurations\n\nTo rigorously attribute perplexity shifts specifically to runtime clipping:\n\n1. **Unquantized FP16 Baseline ($B_0$):**\n   - Full 16-bit precision for weights, activations, and KV cache.\n   - Provides the empirical lower bound on perplexity ($PPL_{\\text{FP16}}$).\n2. **Unclipped Quantized Baseline ($B_{\\text{unclipped}}, \\alpha = 1.00$):**\n   - Symmetric dynamic input quantization + asymmetric KV cache quantization with no clipping ($\\alpha = 1.00$).\n   - Quantifies the raw degradation from full-range quantization before any step-size compression.\n3. **Modality Ablations:**\n   - **Ablation 1 (Inputs Only):** Sweep $\\alpha_X \\in [0.70, 1.00]$ while keeping KV cache unquantized (FP16).\n   - **Ablation 2 (KV Cache Only):** Sweep $\\alpha_{KV} \\in [0.70, 1.00]$ while keeping inputs unquantized (FP16).\n4. **Symmetric vs. Asymmetric KV Cache Ablation:**\n   - Replace asymmetric KV cache quantization with symmetric KV cache quantization under the same $\\alpha$ grid to quantify the exact benefit of tracking the zero-point ($Z_{KV}$).\n5. **Bit-Width Configurations:**\n   - Configuration A: **W16A8KV4** (FP16 weights, INT8 symmetric inputs, INT4 asymmetric KV cache) — practical standard maximizing memory savings.\n   - Configuration B: **W16A4KV4** (FP16 weights, INT4 symmetric inputs, INT4 asymmetric KV cache) — extreme low-bit stress testing.\n   - Configuration C: **W16A8KV8** (INT8 symmetric inputs, INT8 asymmetric KV cache) — high-fidelity baseline.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Evaluation Protocol, Benchmark Datasets, and Sequence Parameters\n\n### 5.1 Datasets\n1. **WikiText-2 (Test Split):**\n   - Standard NLP language modeling benchmark containing long-range contextual dependencies and diverse vocabulary.\n   - Size: 288,000 tokens evaluated using non-overlapping contiguous chunks.\n2. **C4 (Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus, `en` Validation Split):**\n   - Diverse, realistic web domain text to assess out-of-distribution robustness.\n   - Evaluation sample: Randomly selected 256 contiguous chunks of 2048 tokens ($524,288$ tokens total) with a fixed seed.\n\n### 5.2 Sequence Length & Autoregressive Serving Protocol\n- **Sequence Context Length ($L$):** $L = 2048$ tokens.\n- **Decoding Mechanism:** Autoregressive causal language modeling. At step $t$, the full KV cache history for steps $1 \\dots t-1$ is quantized in memory, retrieved, dequantized, and fed into the scaled dot-product attention:\n  $$\\text{Attention}(Q, \\hat{K}, \\hat{V}) = \\text{Softmax}\\left(\\frac{Q \\hat{K}^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) \\hat{V}$$\n- **Primary Metric Formulation:**\n  For a token sequence $\\mathbf{x} = (x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_N)$:\n  $$\\mathcal{L} = -\\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\ln P_{\\text{model}}(x_i \\mid x_{<i})$$\n  $$\\text{PPL} = \\exp(\\mathcal{L})$$\n\n---\n\n## 6. Analytical Framework & Optimal Clipping Ratio Determination\n\n### 6.1 Theoretical Trade-Off: Rounding vs. Clipping Error\nFor a signal $x$ distributed with probability density $p(x)$ under dynamic threshold $T(\\alpha) = \\alpha \\cdot X_{\\max}$:\n\n1. **Quantization Rounding Error ($\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{round}}$):**\n   Within $[-T(\\alpha), T(\\alpha)]$, the step size is $\\Delta(\\alpha) = \\frac{2 T(\\alpha)}{2^b - 1} \\propto \\alpha$.\n   The uniform rounding noise variance is:\n   $$\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{round}}(\\alpha) \\approx \\frac{\\Delta(\\alpha)^2}{12} \\int_{-T(\\alpha)}^{T(\\alpha)} p(x) dx \\propto \\alpha^2$$\n   As $\\alpha$ decreases, $\\Delta$ contracts, yielding higher numerical precision for the central $>99\\%$ of values.\n\n2. **Clipping Truncation Error ($\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{clip}}$):**\n   Values outside $[-T(\\alpha), T(\\alpha)]$ are saturated:\n   $$\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{clip}}(\\alpha) = 2 \\int_{T(\\alpha)}^{\\infty} (x - T(\\alpha))^2 p(x) dx$$\n   As $\\alpha \\to 0$, extreme outlier mass is heavily distorted, which severely corrupts attention softmax scores (attention sinks and sharp activation spikes).\n\n3. **Total Quantization MSE & Perplexity Profile:**\n   $$\\text{MSE}(\\alpha) = \\mathcal{E}_{\\text{round}}(\\alpha) + \\mathcal{E}_{\\text{clip}}(\\alpha)$$\n   Because $\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{round}}(\\alpha)$ is strictly increasing in $\\alpha$ and $\\mathcal{E}_{\\text{clip}}(\\alpha)$ is strictly decreasing in $\\alpha$, their sum forms a **strictly convex U-shaped curve**.\n   The optimal fixed clipping ratio is defined as:\n   $$\\alpha^* = \\arg\\min_{\\alpha \\in [0.70, 1.00]} \\text{PPL}(\\alpha)$$\n\n### 6.2 Structural Diagnostics\n- **Layer-wise Signal-to-Quantization-Noise Ratio (SQNR):**\n  $$\\text{SQNR}_l = 10 \\log_{10} \\frac{\\|Y_l\\|_2^2}{\\|Y_l - \\hat{Y}_l\\|_2^2}$$\n  Tracks whether optimal $\\alpha^*$ aligns with maximum SQNR in early layers (e.g. layers 1–4, where massive attention sink outliers concentrate) vs. deep layers.\n- **Softmax Entropy Divergence:**\n  $$\\mathcal{D}_{KL}\\left(\\text{Softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) \\Big\\|\\, \\text{Softmax}\\left(\\frac{Q\\hat{K}^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)\\right)$$\n  Monitors whether aggressive KV clipping breaks attention focus.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Practical Feasibility & Implementation Considerations\n\n1. **Runtime Overhead:**\n   Dynamic scaling and clipping are fused directly into the memory-read/write CUDA or Triton kernels (e.g., FlashAttention-compatible custom dequant kernels). Calculating $T = \\alpha \\cdot \\max(|X|)$ requires a single reduction pass per token/vector, introducing virtually zero latency overhead compared to unclipped dynamic quantization.\n2. **Numerical Safeguards:**\n   - Small epsilon $\\epsilon = 10^{-7}$ added to ranges to prevent division-by-zero during scale computation.\n   - Zero-points strictly clamped to $[0, 2^{b_{KV}}-1]$ to eliminate integer overflow during memory packing.\n3. **Reproducibility & Rigor:**\n   - Execution is strictly prohibited in this design phase. All parameters, seeds, sequence boundaries, and mathematical mappings are deterministically specified to guarantee end-to-end reproducibility when executed on GPU hardware (e.g., NVIDIA A100/H100).", "evidence": "Researcher confirmed focus on LLAMA2-7B (setup_1), perplexity evaluation (setup_17), and asymmetric KV cache quantization alongside symmetric input quantization (setup_10). Model architecture specifications for LLAMA2-7B (32 layers, hidden size 4096, 32 heads) and standard autoregressive evaluation conventions are well-established in the literature.", "assumptions": ["Weights remain in FP16 precision (or fixed uniform baseline) so that perplexity fluctuations directly measure input and KV cache quantization effects.", "The clipping operation contracts the dynamic range symmetrically around the tensor/vector mean or midpoint, scaling min-max bounds by alpha.", "Tokens are processed autoregressively with full past KV history maintained in the quantized KV cache up to context length L=2048.", "WikiText-2 (test split) and C4 (validation split) serve as the benchmark corpora for causal language modeling perplexity."], "uncertainties": ["Empirical value of the exact minimum alpha* cannot be known without hardware execution, but is theoretically bounded within [0.85, 0.98] due to outlier distribution characteristics in LLAMA2-7B.", "Potential hardware-specific speedups from non-power-of-two or fused INT4/INT8 kernel implementations are subject to specific CUDA/Triton runtime backend optimization."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call_773756__thought__", "tool_call": {"id": "call_773756__thought__", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Complete experiment design for evaluating clipping ratio effects on LLAMA2-7B with symmetric input and asymmetric KV cache quantization\", \"uncertainties\": [\"Empirical value of the exact minimum alpha* cannot be known without hardware execution, but is theoretically bounded within [0.85, 0.98] due to outlier distribution characteristics in LLAMA2-7B.\", \"Potential hardware-specific speedups from non-power-of-two or fused INT4/INT8 kernel implementations are subject to specific CUDA/Triton runtime backend optimization.\"], \"answer\": \"# Comprehensive Experiment Design: Impact of Runtime Clipping Ratio on LLAMA2-7B Quantization Perplexity\\n\\n## 1. Executive Summary & Experimental Objectives\\nThis experiment designs a rigorous, controlled protocol to evaluate how runtime clipping ratio ($\\\\alpha$) affects language modeling perplexity (PPL) on **LLAMA2-7B** under a hybrid quantization scheme: **symmetric dynamic input (activation) quantization** and **asymmetric KV cache quantization**.\\n\\nThe core objective is to map the trade-off curve between quantization rounding noise (which decreases as the representation range contracts) and clipping saturation distortion (which increases as extreme values are truncated), thereby determining the optimal fixed clipping ratio $\\\\alpha^*$ that minimizes perplexity during autoregressive inference.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Mathematical Formulation of Quantization and Clipping\\n\\n### 2.1 Model Specifications\\n- **Target Model:** LLAMA2-7B (`meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf`).\\n  - Architecture: 32 Transformer layers, hidden dimension $d = 4096$, intermediate dimension $11008$, 32 attention heads ($d_h = 128$), RoPE positional embeddings, SwiGLU non-linearities, Grouped Query Attention (GQA) is standard MHA in 7B.\\n  - Base Precision for Weights: Kept in original FP16 (or optionally fixed unclipped W8A8 baseline) to isolate the causal impact of activation and KV cache quantization.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.2 Symmetric Input Quantization (Dynamic Activations)\\nInput activations $X \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^{B \\\\times L \\\\times d}$ entering linear projections (e.g., $W_q, W_k, W_v, W_o, W_{\\\\text{gate}}, W_{\\\\text{up}}, W_{\\\\text{down}}$) exhibit zero-centered distributions with sparse, large-magnitude outlier channels.\\n\\n1. **Unclipped Dynamic Range:**\\n   $$X_{\\\\max} = \\\\max_{j} |X_j| \\\\quad (\\\\text{per-token or per-tensor})$$\\n2. **Clipped Dynamic Threshold with Clipping Ratio $\\\\alpha_X \\\\in (0, 1]$:**\\n   $$T_X(\\\\alpha_X) = \\\\alpha_X \\\\cdot X_{\\\\max}$$\\n3. **Clamping Operation:**\\n   $$\\\\bar{X} = \\\\text{clamp}(X, -T_X(\\\\alpha_X), T_X(\\\\alpha_X))$$\\n4. **Scale Calculation (Symmetric, Zero-point $Z_X = 0$):**\\n   For bit-width $b_{\\\\text{in}}$ (primary target $b_{\\\\text{in}} = 8$ or $4$, mapped to integer interval $[-2^{b_{\\\\text{in}}-1}, 2^{b_{\\\\text{in}}-1}-1]$ or $[-2^{b_{\\\\text{in}}-1}+1, 2^{b_{\\\\text{in}}-1}-1]$):\\n   $$S_X = \\\\frac{T_X(\\\\alpha_X)}{2^{b_{\\\\text{in}}-1} - 1}$$\\n5. **Quantization and Dequantization:**\\n   $$Q_X(X) = \\\\text{round}\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\bar{X}}{S_X}\\\\right), \\\\quad \\\\hat{X} = Q_X(X) \\\\cdot S_X$$\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.3 Asymmetric KV Cache Quantization\\nKey ($K$) and Value ($V$) tensors stored in the KV cache across decoding steps exhibit non-zero-centered, skewed distributions (e.g., post-RoPE Key vectors and post-activation Value vectors often exhibit asymmetric positive/negative channel bounds). Asymmetric quantization maps the dynamic interval $[V_{\\\\min}, V_{\\\\max}]$ into unsigned integer interval $[0, 2^{b_{KV}} - 1]$.\\n\\n1. **Unclipped Bounds:**\\n   For a given key/value vector (per-head per-token or per-channel vector $Y \\\\in \\\\{K, V\\\\}$):\\n   $$Y_{\\\\min} = \\\\min(Y), \\\\quad Y_{\\\\max} = \\\\max(Y)$$\\n   $$\\\\text{Range}(Y) = Y_{\\\\max} - Y_{\\\\min}, \\\\quad \\\\text{Midpoint}(Y) = \\\\frac{Y_{\\\\max} + Y_{\\\\min}}{2}$$\\n\\n2. **Clipped Dynamic Range with Clipping Ratio $\\\\alpha_{KV} \\\\in (0, 1]$:**\\n   To contract the dynamic range symmetrically around the distribution center:\\n   $$\\\\Delta_{\\\\text{half}} = \\\\alpha_{KV} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{\\\\text{Range}(Y)}{2}$$\\n   $$T_{Y,\\\\min}(\\\\alpha_{KV}) = \\\\text{Midpoint}(Y) - \\\\Delta_{\\\\text{half}}$$\\n   $$T_{Y,\\\\max}(\\\\alpha_{KV}) = \\\\text{Midpoint}(Y) + \\\\Delta_{\\\\text{half}}$$\\n\\n3. **Clamping Operation:**\\n   $$\\\\bar{Y} = \\\\text{clamp}\\\\left(Y, T_{Y,\\\\min}(\\\\alpha_{KV}), T_{Y,\\\\max}(\\\\alpha_{KV})\\\\right)$$\\n\\n4. **Scale and Asymmetric Zero-Point ($Z_{KV}$):**\\n   For bit-width $b_{KV}$ (primary target $b_{KV} = 4$ for high-throughput memory reduction, and $b_{KV} = 8$):\\n   $$S_Y = \\\\frac{T_{Y,\\\\max}(\\\\alpha_{KV}) - T_{Y,\\\\min}(\\\\alpha_{KV})}{2^{b_{KV}} - 1} = \\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{KV} \\\\cdot (Y_{\\\\max} - Y_{\\\\min})}{2^{b_{KV}} - 1}$$\\n   $$Z_Y = \\\\text{round}\\\\left( - \\\\frac{T_{Y,\\\\min}(\\\\alpha_{KV})}{S_Y} \\\\right) = \\\\text{round}\\\\left( \\\\frac{2^{b_{KV}} - 1}{2} - \\\\frac{\\\\text{Midpoint}(Y)}{S_Y} \\\\right)$$\\n   $$Z_Y = \\\\text{clamp}\\\\left(Z_Y, 0, 2^{b_{KV}} - 1\\\\right)$$\\n\\n5. **Quantization and Dequantization:**\\n   $$Q_Y(Y) = \\\\text{clamp}\\\\left(\\\\text{round}\\\\left(\\\\frac{\\\\bar{Y} - T_{Y,\\\\min}}{S_Y}\\\\right), 0, 2^{b_{KV}} - 1\\\\right)$$\\n   $$\\\\hat{Y} = S_Y \\\\cdot (Q_Y(Y) - Z_Y)$$\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Clipping Ratio Sweep Design & Parameter Justification\\n\\n### 3.1 Candidate Grid and Resolution\\nTo isolate the convex minimum $\\\\alpha^*$ without overlooking boundary phenomena, we formulate a two-tier dense grid:\\n\\n| Regime | Ratio Values ($\\\\alpha$) | Step Size ($\\\\Delta \\\\alpha$) | Justification |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Coarse Lower Bound** | $0.70, 0.75, 0.80$ | $0.05$ | Tests catastrophic saturation threshold where clipping truncation dominates. |\\n| **Fine Search Region** | $0.85, 0.88, 0.90, 0.92, 0.94, 0.95, 0.96, 0.98$ | $0.01 - 0.02$ | High-sensitivity region where optimal trade-off between outlier preservation and step-size reduction occurs. |\\n| **Upper Bound (Control)** | $1.00$ | \\u2014 | Standard unclipped min-max quantization baseline. |\\n\\n### 3.2 Coupling Strategy\\n1. **Primary Protocol (Joint Fixed Ratio):**\\n   Apply a uniform global clipping ratio $\\\\alpha = \\\\alpha_X = \\\\alpha_{KV}$ across both input activations and KV cache. This directly addresses the research question of identifying a single optimal fixed runtime parameter.\\n2. **Factorial Secondary Protocol (Decoupled Grid):**\\n   A $4 \\\\times 4$ factorial sweep testing $\\\\alpha_X \\\\in \\\\{0.85, 0.90, 0.95, 1.00\\\\}$ against $\\\\alpha_{KV} \\\\in \\\\{0.85, 0.90, 0.95, 1.00\\\\}$ to determine whether activation inputs or KV cache representations are more sensitive to saturation vs rounding noise.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Baselines, Controls, and Ablation Configurations\\n\\nTo rigorously attribute perplexity shifts specifically to runtime clipping:\\n\\n1. **Unquantized FP16 Baseline ($B_0$):**\\n   - Full 16-bit precision for weights, activations, and KV cache.\\n   - Provides the empirical lower bound on perplexity ($PPL_{\\\\text{FP16}}$).\\n2. **Unclipped Quantized Baseline ($B_{\\\\text{unclipped}}, \\\\alpha = 1.00$):**\\n   - Symmetric dynamic input quantization + asymmetric KV cache quantization with no clipping ($\\\\alpha = 1.00$).\\n   - Quantifies the raw degradation from full-range quantization before any step-size compression.\\n3. **Modality Ablations:**\\n   - **Ablation 1 (Inputs Only):** Sweep $\\\\alpha_X \\\\in [0.70, 1.00]$ while keeping KV cache unquantized (FP16).\\n   - **Ablation 2 (KV Cache Only):** Sweep $\\\\alpha_{KV} \\\\in [0.70, 1.00]$ while keeping inputs unquantized (FP16).\\n4. **Symmetric vs. Asymmetric KV Cache Ablation:**\\n   - Replace asymmetric KV cache quantization with symmetric KV cache quantization under the same $\\\\alpha$ grid to quantify the exact benefit of tracking the zero-point ($Z_{KV}$).\\n5. **Bit-Width Configurations:**\\n   - Configuration A: **W16A8KV4** (FP16 weights, INT8 symmetric inputs, INT4 asymmetric KV cache) \\u2014 practical standard maximizing memory savings.\\n   - Configuration B: **W16A4KV4** (FP16 weights, INT4 symmetric inputs, INT4 asymmetric KV cache) \\u2014 extreme low-bit stress testing.\\n   - Configuration C: **W16A8KV8** (INT8 symmetric inputs, INT8 asymmetric KV cache) \\u2014 high-fidelity baseline.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Evaluation Protocol, Benchmark Datasets, and Sequence Parameters\\n\\n### 5.1 Datasets\\n1. **WikiText-2 (Test Split):**\\n   - Standard NLP language modeling benchmark containing long-range contextual dependencies and diverse vocabulary.\\n   - Size: 288,000 tokens evaluated using non-overlapping contiguous chunks.\\n2. **C4 (Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus, `en` Validation Split):**\\n   - Diverse, realistic web domain text to assess out-of-distribution robustness.\\n   - Evaluation sample: Randomly selected 256 contiguous chunks of 2048 tokens ($524,288$ tokens total) with a fixed seed.\\n\\n### 5.2 Sequence Length & Autoregressive Serving Protocol\\n- **Sequence Context Length ($L$):** $L = 2048$ tokens.\\n- **Decoding Mechanism:** Autoregressive causal language modeling. At step $t$, the full KV cache history for steps $1 \\\\dots t-1$ is quantized in memory, retrieved, dequantized, and fed into the scaled dot-product attention:\\n  $$\\\\text{Attention}(Q, \\\\hat{K}, \\\\hat{V}) = \\\\text{Softmax}\\\\left(\\\\frac{Q \\\\hat{K}^T}{\\\\sqrt{d_k}}\\\\right) \\\\hat{V}$$\\n- **Primary Metric Formulation:**\\n  For a token sequence $\\\\mathbf{x} = (x_1, x_2, \\\\dots, x_N)$:\\n  $$\\\\mathcal{L} = -\\\\frac{1}{N} \\\\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\\\ln P_{\\\\text{model}}(x_i \\\\mid x_{<i})$$\\n  $$\\\\text{PPL} = \\\\exp(\\\\mathcal{L})$$\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Analytical Framework & Optimal Clipping Ratio Determination\\n\\n### 6.1 Theoretical Trade-Off: Rounding vs. Clipping Error\\nFor a signal $x$ distributed with probability density $p(x)$ under dynamic threshold $T(\\\\alpha) = \\\\alpha \\\\cdot X_{\\\\max}$:\\n\\n1. **Quantization Rounding Error ($\\\\mathcal{E}_{\\\\text{round}}$):**\\n   Within $[-T(\\\\alpha), T(\\\\alpha)]$, the step size is $\\\\Delta(\\\\alpha) = \\\\frac{2 T(\\\\alpha)}{2^b - 1} \\\\propto \\\\alpha$.\\n   The uniform rounding noise variance is:\\n   $$\\\\mathcal{E}_{\\\\text{round}}(\\\\alpha) \\\\approx \\\\frac{\\\\Delta(\\\\alpha)^2}{12} \\\\int_{-T(\\\\alpha)}^{T(\\\\alpha)} p(x) dx \\\\propto \\\\alpha^2$$\\n   As $\\\\alpha$ decreases, $\\\\Delta$ contracts, yielding higher numerical precision for the central $>99\\\\%$ of values.\\n\\n2. **Clipping Truncation Error ($\\\\mathcal{E}_{\\\\text{clip}}$):**\\n   Values outside $[-T(\\\\alpha), T(\\\\alpha)]$ are saturated:\\n   $$\\\\mathcal{E}_{\\\\text{clip}}(\\\\alpha) = 2 \\\\int_{T(\\\\alpha)}^{\\\\infty} (x - T(\\\\alpha))^2 p(x) dx$$\\n   As $\\\\alpha \\\\to 0$, extreme outlier mass is heavily distorted, which severely corrupts attention softmax scores (attention sinks and sharp activation spikes).\\n\\n3. **Total Quantization MSE & Perplexity Profile:**\\n   $$\\\\text{MSE}(\\\\alpha) = \\\\mathcal{E}_{\\\\text{round}}(\\\\alpha) + \\\\mathcal{E}_{\\\\text{clip}}(\\\\alpha)$$\\n   Because $\\\\mathcal{E}_{\\\\text{round}}(\\\\alpha)$ is strictly increasing in $\\\\alpha$ and $\\\\mathcal{E}_{\\\\text{clip}}(\\\\alpha)$ is strictly decreasing in $\\\\alpha$, their sum forms a **strictly convex U-shaped curve**.\\n   The optimal fixed clipping ratio is defined as:\\n   $$\\\\alpha^* = \\\\arg\\\\min_{\\\\alpha \\\\in [0.70, 1.00]} \\\\text{PPL}(\\\\alpha)$$\\n\\n### 6.2 Structural Diagnostics\\n- **Layer-wise Signal-to-Quantization-Noise Ratio (SQNR):**\\n  $$\\\\text{SQNR}_l = 10 \\\\log_{10} \\\\frac{\\\\|Y_l\\\\|_2^2}{\\\\|Y_l - \\\\hat{Y}_l\\\\|_2^2}$$\\n  Tracks whether optimal $\\\\alpha^*$ aligns with maximum SQNR in early layers (e.g. layers 1\\u20134, where massive attention sink outliers concentrate) vs. deep layers.\\n- **Softmax Entropy Divergence:**\\n  $$\\\\mathcal{D}_{KL}\\\\left(\\\\text{Softmax}\\\\left(\\\\frac{QK^T}{\\\\sqrt{d_k}}\\\\right) \\\\Big\\\\|\\\\, \\\\text{Softmax}\\\\left(\\\\frac{Q\\\\hat{K}^T}{\\\\sqrt{d_k}}\\\\right)\\\\right)$$\\n  Monitors whether aggressive KV clipping breaks attention focus.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Practical Feasibility & Implementation Considerations\\n\\n1. **Runtime Overhead:**\\n   Dynamic scaling and clipping are fused directly into the memory-read/write CUDA or Triton kernels (e.g., FlashAttention-compatible custom dequant kernels). Calculating $T = \\\\alpha \\\\cdot \\\\max(|X|)$ requires a single reduction pass per token/vector, introducing virtually zero latency overhead compared to unclipped dynamic quantization.\\n2. **Numerical Safeguards:**\\n   - Small epsilon $\\\\epsilon = 10^{-7}$ added to ranges to prevent division-by-zero during scale computation.\\n   - Zero-points strictly clamped to $[0, 2^{b_{KV}}-1]$ to eliminate integer overflow during memory packing.\\n3. **Reproducibility & Rigor:**\\n   - Execution is strictly prohibited in this design phase. All parameters, seeds, sequence boundaries, and mathematical mappings are deterministically specified to guarantee end-to-end reproducibility when executed on GPU hardware (e.g., NVIDIA A100/H100).\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"assumptions\": [\"Weights remain in FP16 precision (or fixed uniform baseline) so that perplexity fluctuations directly measure input and KV cache quantization effects.\", \"The clipping operation contracts the dynamic range symmetrically around the tensor/vector mean or midpoint, scaling min-max bounds by alpha.\", \"Tokens are processed autoregressively with full past KV history maintained in the quantized KV cache up to context length L=2048.\", \"WikiText-2 (test split) and C4 (validation split) serve as the benchmark corpora for causal language modeling perplexity.\"], \"evidence\": \"Researcher confirmed focus on LLAMA2-7B (setup_1), perplexity evaluation (setup_17), and asymmetric KV cache quantization alongside symmetric input quantization (setup_10). Model architecture specifications for LLAMA2-7B (32 layers, hidden size 4096, 32 heads) and standard autoregressive evaluation conventions are well-established in the literature.\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "e8fUaoqKM5OhpucP1ZOZ8Aw", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 17}
